#---
# purpose: "negating a value that may be an integer or a float works on every door, like every other operator"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # negate_dynamic
#
# Every BINARY arithmetic operator is a typed clause family in `Kernel`, so a
# dynamically-typed operand dispatches on its runtime type. Unary minus was
# lowered straight to one machine instruction and read a single lane, so it
# aborted natively on exactly the values subtraction handled fine:
#
#     0 - pick(0)     interp -4.5   run -4.5   build -4.5
#     -pick(0)        interp -4.5   run ABORT  build ABORT
#
# Subtracting from zero worked and negating did not. `-x` is now
# `Kernel.negate(x)`, which makes the two agree by construction.
#
# A JSON number parser is exactly this shape: it negates a value that is an
# integer or a float depending on the text it just scanned.
#
# ## What it covers
#
#   * negation of a union-typed value, both arms;
#   * negation through a function whose parameter is untyped, so the lane
#     cannot be known at the call;
#   * `-0.0`, which must stay `-0.0`. This is why `-x` is NOT lowered as
#     `0 - x`: that answers `0.0`, and the sign of zero is observable;
#   * negative literals beside them, which fold to literals rather than calls.
defp pick(0), do: 4.5
defp pick(_), do: 9

defp negate_any(x), do: -x

def main() do
  dbg(-pick(0))
  dbg(-pick(1))
  dbg(negate_any(2.5))
  dbg(negate_any(7))
  dbg(negate_any(0.0))
  dbg(-0.0)
  dbg(-3)
  dbg(-3.5)
end
